\section{Experiments}\label{sec:experiments}

We evaluate temporal probes across four reasoning models (\cref{tab:models}), comparing hidden-state (HS) and text modalities under different layer selection criteria.

\subsection{Setup}\label{sec:setup}

We study R1-8B (Llama-8B, R1-distilled, $n{=}43$ test traces), OT-7B (Qwen-7B, STILL-trained, $n{=}47$), QwQ-32B (Qwen-32B, native reasoning, $n{=}19$--$20$), and R1-32B (Qwen-32B, R1-distilled, $n{=}20$). All traces come from HarmThoughts with step-level CP annotations (60/20/20 split). HS probes extract last-token hidden states from a selected layer; text probes encode each step with all-MiniLM-L6-v2 (384d). Both use L2-regularized logistic regression with temporal windowing $W \in \{1, 3, 5, 10, 15, 20, 25\}$ selected on validation. Detection requires $K{=}5$ consecutive threshold crossings. Statistical testing follows \cref{sec:evaluation}.

\subsection{Layer Selection Results}\label{sec:layer-results}

\cref{tab:layer_selection} shows that the biased composite metric (balanced accuracy $\times$ crossing rate) systematically selects shallow layers for 32B models: L2 (3\% depth) for QwQ-32B and L10 (16\%) for R1-32B. All metrics excluding crossing rate consistently select deep layers: L60 (94\%) and L63 (98\%). The mechanism is that shallow layers produce highly compressed representations with small inter-step margins: step-to-step cosine distances are 1.4--2.7$\times$ smaller than at deep layers across all four models (\cref{app:cosine_volatility}). A linear probe in this compressed space operates near the noise floor, generating frequent threshold crossings that inflate crossing rate independently of discriminative power (\cref{sec:layer-sensitivity}).

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\begin{tabular}{lccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Model} & \textbf{Biased}$^a$ & \textbf{Corrected}$^b$ & $p$ \textbf{(corr.)} \\
\midrule
R1-8B & L14 (44\%) & L14 (44\%) & ${<}0.001$ \\
OT-7B & L0 (0\%) & L16 (57\%) & ${<}0.001$ \\
QwQ-32B & L2 (3\%) & L60 (94\%) & ${<}0.005$ \\
R1-32B & L10 (16\%) & L63 (98\%) & ${<}0.001$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{Layer selection sensitivity. $^a$\,bal\_acc $\times$ crossing\_rate. $^b$\,Consensus of all metrics excluding crossing rate. Rightmost: HS vs.\ text precision $p$-value at corrected layer (e.g., QwQ-32B biased-layer: bal\_acc $p{=}0.35$, precision $p{=}0.83$, both non-significant).}
\label{tab:layer_selection}
\end{table}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig_layer_heatmap.pdf}
\caption{Per-layer metric heatmap (2$\times$2). Each column: one of five metrics (min-max normalized). Red dashed: biased layer (bal\_acc $\times$ crossing\_rate); green dashed: corrected layer (pure balanced accuracy). For R1-8B, both coincide at L14. At 32B, biased selections cluster near input layers while all other metrics peak at deep layers.}
\label{fig:layer_heatmap}
\end{figure}

The downstream impact is severe: at biased layers, only the two 7--8B models show significant HS advantage; both 32B models fail. At corrected layers, all four models are significant, each on three correlated metrics (Holm-Bonferroni corrected, \cref{sec:evaluation}). Had we reported only biased-layer results, we would have concluded a scale-dependent HS advantage that is entirely a methodological artifact.

\subsection{HS vs.\ Text Comparison}\label{sec:hs-text}

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{%
\begin{tabular}{llclrrlrc}
\toprule
\textbf{Model} & \textbf{Layer} & \textbf{Enc.} & $W$ & $\Delta$\textbf{BA} & $\Delta$\textbf{Prec} & \textbf{95\% CI} & $\Delta$\textbf{FPR} & \textbf{HB} \\
\midrule
R1-8B & L14 & 384d & 15 & +8.2 & \textbf{+32.5} & [+20.2, +45.2] & $-$7.4 & 3/3 \\
 & & 1024d & & \multicolumn{1}{c}{--} & +5.8 & [$-$3.0, +15.0] & \multicolumn{1}{c}{--} & 0/1 \\[2pt]
OT-7B & L16 & 384d & 3 & +10.7 & +21.9 & [+14.4, +29.6] & $-$17.1 & 3/3 \\
 & & 1024d & & \multicolumn{1}{c}{--} & +18.1 & [+9.0, +27.0] & \multicolumn{1}{c}{--} & 1/1 \\[2pt]
QwQ & L60 & 384d & 3 & +15.4 & +23.6 & [+12.9, +35.1] & $-$9.8 & 3/3 \\
 & & 1024d & & \multicolumn{1}{c}{--} & +9.4 & [$-$3.0, +22.0] & \multicolumn{1}{c}{--} & 0/1 \\[2pt]
R1-32B & L63 & 384d & 1 & \textbf{+18.7} & +15.4 & [+8.7, +22.6] & \textbf{$-$14.6} & 3/3 \\
 & & 1024d & & \multicolumn{1}{c}{--} & +11.7 & [+1.0, +22.0] & \multicolumn{1}{c}{--} & 0/1 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}}
\caption{HS vs.\ text at corrected layers (LR probes, 60/20/20 split). Enc.: text encoder (384d = MiniLM-L6-v2, 1024d = BGE-large). $\Delta$: HS$-$text difference (pp). 95\% CI: bootstrap interval for $\Delta$Prec (10{,}000 resamples). 384d: all 4 models significant, each on 3 correlated metrics (HB corrected, $\alpha{=}0.05$). 1024d: HB over 4 model-level precision tests. Mean values from canonical split; 5-seed mean$\pm$std in \cref{app:multi_seed}. BGE $\Delta$BA/$\Delta$FPR not computed.}
\label{tab:hs_vs_text}
\end{table}

Against the 384d baseline, HS probes significantly outperform text probes on all four models across all three metrics (\cref{tab:hs_vs_text}). At 7--8B, the evidence is strong: precision gains of +32.5pp (R1-8B) and +21.9pp (OT-7B), stable across seeds (std ${<}4$pp). At 32B, both models are significant (HB corrected) but with model-specific caveats: R1-32B achieves significance only at $W{=}1$ with 25\% coverage, and QwQ-32B's position baseline is competitive (\cref{sec:32b-analysis}). Control task selectivity confirms genuine safety signal: 12.5--19.7pp above chance (\cref{app:control}).

\paragraph{Dimensionality control.} PCA reduction of HS features to 384d (matching text encoder dimensionality, 79--92\% variance retained) preserves the precision advantage on all four models (+16.8 to +23.1pp, all $p{<}0.012$), with all four model-level precision tests passing Holm-Bonferroni. Two individual metric comparisons are non-significant: R1-8B balanced accuracy (+1.3pp, $p{=}0.731$, effect too small at $n{=}43$) and QwQ-32B FPR ($-$11.4pp, $p{=}0.038$, below HB threshold). This confirms a modality effect rather than a capacity artifact.

\paragraph{Encoder robustness.} The 1024d rows in \cref{tab:hs_vs_text} replace MiniLM (384d) with BGE-large (1024d, a 2.7$\times$ stronger encoder). The HS precision advantage persists directionally on all four models but narrows substantially: only OT-7B survives Holm-Bonferroni (1/4 tests). The advantage is encoder-conditional rather than absolute. Layer selection (Contribution 1) is encoder-independent, as it concerns hidden-state properties across layers rather than the text baseline.

\paragraph{Caveats.} R1-32B achieves significance only at $W{=}1$ with 25\% coverage (5/20 traces); at $W{\geq}3$, dimensionality ($51200 \times W$ for $n{=}20$) prevents significance. Sweeping regularization $C$ over $[0.01, 100]$ yields ${<}2$pp variation in balanced accuracy, which confirms robustness to hyperparameter choice. QwQ-32B HS lead time is negative ($-$1.2 to +1.9 steps vs.\ text 3.7--12.3 steps): higher precision at later detection. On benign traces, probes produce 39--52\% false alarm rates, so current methods characterize the commitment phenomenon rather than constitute production-ready detectors. Per-model details in \cref{sec:32b-analysis}.

\subsection{32B Model-Specific Analysis}\label{sec:32b-analysis}

R1-32B and QwQ-32B share the Qwen-32B architecture (64 layers, 5120 hidden) but present opposite detection profiles (\cref{tab:32b_divergence}), making training method the likely differentiator.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\begin{tabular}{lcccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Model} & \textbf{Cov.} & \textbf{Lead (steps)} & \textbf{HB} & \textbf{Pos.\ margin} \\
\midrule
R1-32B & 25\% & +11.7 to +13.3 & 3/3 ($W{=}1$) & +14.2pp \\
QwQ-32B & 74\% & $-$1.2 to +1.9 & 3/3 (all $W$) & $-$3.6pp \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{32B cross-model divergence. R1-32B provides early warning with narrow coverage; QwQ-32B provides robust significance with poor lead time. Pos.\ margin: HS balanced accuracy minus position-only baseline.}
\label{tab:32b_divergence}
\end{table}

R1-32B detects commitment 11--13 steps early with a +14.2pp margin over the position baseline, which confirms genuine representational signal, but covers only 25\% of traces at the single significant window ($W{=}1$). QwQ-32B achieves statistical significance at all window sizes with 74\% coverage, but HS probes fire later than text probes (negative lead time). The position baseline achieves higher balanced accuracy than HS probes (0.832 vs.\ 0.796) because harmful steps tend to occur later in traces; however, HS probes achieve higher precision (+4.5pp: 0.564 vs.\ 0.519) and lower FPR ($-$3.0pp: 0.217 vs.\ 0.247), which indicates that hidden states capture safety-specific patterns beyond positional information. The divergence implies that per-model validation is essential: no single detection configuration generalizes even within the same architecture at the same scale, and a system validated on one 32B model would fail on the other.

\subsection{Temporal Context}\label{sec:temporal}

Temporal windowing ($W{>}1$) improves both modalities on all four models (\cref{fig:window_ablation}). Text precision improves +8 to +23pp from $W{=}1$ to $W{=}25$; HS precision improves +1.7 to +12.9pp. At 7--8B, the modality advantage (+22 to +33pp precision) exceeds the temporal improvement (+13pp). Both effects are complementary: the most robust deployment default is temporal windowing ($W{=}3$--$15$) combined with principled layer selection. Full per-model temporal ablations appear in \cref{app:temporal_details}.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig2_temporal_improvement.pdf}
\caption{Temporal window effect on R1-8B HS probe (L14). Precision: 69.3\% ($W{=}1$) to 82.2\% ($W{=}25$); FPR: 9.65\% to 4.8\%.}
\label{fig:window_ablation}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Negative Results}\label{sec:negative}

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\begin{tabular}{p{1.5cm}p{2.9cm}p{2.3cm}}
\toprule
\textbf{Method} & \textbf{Result} & \textbf{Limitation} \\
\midrule
BOCPD & 0\% det.\ on 3/4 models & Commitment gradual \\
CUSUM & $<$ threshold, all models & Same as BOCPD \\
Mahalanobis & $\Delta d \approx 0$ & Signal in minor PCs \\
RepE & QwQ 0.756; R1-32B 0.559$^a$ & Model-dependent \\
MLP & 3/4 worse than LR & Overfitting \\
CCS & No gain over supervised$^b$ & Alignment mismatch \\
Transfer & Best 59.5\% (PCA)$^c$ & Model-specific repr. \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{Seven alternative approaches. $^a$\,RepE QwQ approaches supervised bal\_acc but precision is 0.512 vs.\ LR 0.700. $^b$\,Semi-supervised (requires CP labels). $^c$\,Best direct transfer 67.3\% but 1\% crossing rate.}
\label{tab:negative}
\end{table}

No alternative method consistently matches supervised probing across all four models (\cref{tab:negative}). We prioritize precision over balanced accuracy as the primary evaluation criterion because false positives on pre-CP steps waste intervention resources (triggering unnecessary safety responses), while missed post-CP steps merely reduce coverage without causing immediate harm. This framing is critical for interpreting RepE results below.

\paragraph{Change-point methods.} BOCPD~\citep{adams2007bayesian} achieves 0\% detection on 3/4 models because safety commitment is gradual rather than abrupt: the probe score sequence transitions smoothly over 5--15 steps without the discontinuity that BOCPD's hazard function requires. On R1-8B (the sole model with non-zero detection), the best BOCPD configuration ($\lambda{=}10$) achieves 93\% detection rate but with lower lead time (+3.5 steps) than simple thresholding (+4.9 steps). CUSUM similarly fails because cumulative evidence never exceeds the decision boundary on smooth transitions. The gradual nature of commitment is itself an empirical finding: it rules out the entire class of abrupt-change detectors and motivates the supervised probe approach that explicitly learns the transition boundary.

\paragraph{RepE inconsistency.} RepE mean-difference directions~\citep{zou2023representation} illustrate the metric-dependent nature of method comparison. On QwQ-32B, RepE achieves 0.756 balanced accuracy (comparable to supervised LR at 0.778), but its precision is 0.512 vs.\ LR 0.700 and its FPR is 0.309 vs.\ 0.120. The mean-difference direction captures enough signal to classify steps above chance, but fires indiscriminately on pre-CP steps, producing 2.6$\times$ more false alarms than supervised probes. On R1-32B, RepE nearly fails entirely (0.559 balanced accuracy). The inconsistency across two models sharing the same architecture confirms that a single linear direction computed from class means does not reliably capture safety commitment structure.

Cross-model transfer produces no practical utility across 17 configurations; stronger alignment methods (CCA, model stitching~\cite{kornblith2019cka,bansal2021stitching}) remain unexplored. Detailed per-method analysis in \cref{app:negative_details}.

\section{Discussion}\label{sec:discussion}

\paragraph{Layer selection as researcher degree of freedom.}
Layer selection is an underexamined methodological choice in the probing literature that can silently determine a study's conclusions. Our five-metric ablation (\cref{sec:layer-results}) demonstrates that a single metric component, the crossing rate, reverses the central finding of an HS vs.\ text comparison: at biased layers, only the two 7--8B models show significant HS advantage, which produces an apparent scale-dependent modality effect; at corrected layers, all four models are significant (Holm-Bonferroni corrected, \cref{sec:evaluation}). Step-to-step cosine distance analysis (\cref{app:cosine_volatility}) reveals the mechanism: shallow representations are highly compressed, with inter-step cosine distances 1.4--2.7$\times$ smaller than at deep layers. A linear probe in this compressed space operates near the noise floor, so threshold crossings are noise-driven rather than reflecting genuine semantic transitions. Any composite metric that incorporates crossing rate is susceptible to this bias. This bias is not specific to safety probing. We hypothesize that any threshold-based detection study that uses crossing rate as a layer selection criterion, whether for sentiment, factuality, or toxicity, could produce analogous reversals. Validation on other probing tasks is needed to confirm generality.

\paragraph{Recommendations for probing methodology.} We recommend that probing studies report results under at least two complementary layer selection criteria, and that five-metric ablations (or at minimum, results at multiple candidate layers spanning shallow, middle, and deep positions) become standard practice. Even among unbiased metrics, the choice between threshold-based and threshold-free criteria affects layer selection: AUROC tends to select layers at shallower depth (3--50\%) than threshold-dependent metrics (44--98\%), further demonstrating that layer selection methodology is a critical researcher degree of freedom. At AUROC-optimal layers, HS probes retain directional precision advantage on all four models (+6.2 to +23.0pp), though these layers yield lower absolute performance than the deep layers selected by threshold-dependent metrics. The cost of ablation is modest: evaluating five metrics across all layers adds minutes to an experiment that takes hours. The alternative is that conclusions about representational structure reflect layer selection methodology rather than the phenomenon under study. The control task provides independent corroboration: R1-32B selectivity increases from 7.5pp at the biased layer (L10) to 19.7pp at the corrected layer (L63), which confirms that the biased metric selected layers where positional features dominate over genuine safety structure.

\paragraph{Temporal context and modality choice.}
Information-theoretic reasoning suggests that hidden states (4096 to 5120 dimensions of internal computation) should dominate text features (384-dimensional sentence embeddings) purely by signal capacity. Both temporal context and modality choice significantly improve detection, but their relative magnitudes differ by scale. At 7--8B, the modality advantage exceeds the temporal improvement (\cref{sec:temporal}), so modality choice matters more than temporal context at this scale; at 32B, the relative magnitudes are model-dependent. Text probes benefit more from temporal context than HS probes because they start from a weaker single-step baseline. Safety commitment is gradual: the model progressively consolidates its intent over multiple reasoning steps rather than switching abruptly. The practical implication is that temporal windowing ($W{=}3$ to $15$) is the most robust single default, as it provides guaranteed improvement regardless of modality, while the modality choice requires principled layer selection to realize its full potential.

\paragraph{Cross-model divergence within identical architecture.}
R1-32B and QwQ-32B share the same base architecture but present opposite detection profiles (\cref{tab:32b_divergence}). Training method (R1 distillation vs.\ native reasoning) is the likely differentiator, since architecture and scale are held constant. Per-model validation is essential: a system validated on one 32B model would fail on the other.

\paragraph{Design implications for safety monitoring.}
We recommend temporal windowing ($W{=}3$--$15$) combined with principled layer selection (balanced accuracy or precision, never crossing rate) as deployment defaults. At 7--8B, HS probes Pareto-dominate text probes (\cref{tab:hs_vs_text}). At 32B, practical trade-offs require model-specific decisions (\cref{sec:32b-analysis}). All methods produce high safe-trace FPR (39--52\%), possibly because safe traces contain commitment-like state transitions (e.g., the model deciding to refuse) that probes cannot distinguish from harmful commitment. The 85.5\% oracle gap (\cref{sec:oracle}) confirms that passive monitoring captures a small portion of available signal; hidden states encode the model's current safety state but carry limited predictive power about future trajectory evolution. PCA dimensionality control and control task selectivity provide indirect evidence against textual leakage as the sole explanation for the HS advantage; direct erasure methods (e.g., LEACE~\citep{belrose2023leace}) remain untested.

\section{Related Work}\label{sec:related-work}

\paragraph{Safety in Reasoning Models.}
Reasoning models introduce a qualitatively new failure mode: the model's own chain of thought can transition from safety-aware deliberation to harmful generation without adversarial input modification~\cite{wang2025lrmsafety}. \citet{harmthoughts} document this self-jailbreak phenomenon with step-level annotations, while \citet{selfjailbreak} show that longer reasoning chains increase circumvention probability. \citet{hagendorff2026lrm_jailbreak} demonstrate that reasoning models can autonomously jailbreak other LLMs with 97\% success, identifying reasoning capability itself as a safety threat. H-CoT~\cite{hcot2025} shows reasoning chains can be externally hijacked, complementing internally-originating transitions. Unfaithful reasoning~\cite{chen2025unfaithful} further motivates hidden-state monitoring that bypasses text. On mitigation, SafeThink~\cite{safethink} steers early reasoning toward safety, SafeRemind~\cite{saferemind} inserts entropy-triggered reminders, and \citet{korbak2025cot_monitorability} argue that CoT monitorability is valuable but fragile. \citet{mao2025selfjailbreak_reasoning} further characterize self-jailbreak in reasoning models and propose Chain-of-Guardrails as a defense. \citet{baker2025monitoring} show that CoT monitoring can detect reward hacking in reasoning models but warn that optimization pressure promotes obfuscation, complementing our focus on passive detection. \citet{yin2025refusal_cliff} discover that safety-aligned reasoning models maintain refusal intentions during thinking but experience a sharp drop at output generation, a phenomenon our temporal probes could detect. \citet{ahrend2026safer_traces} address information leakage in traces; \citet{hu2026refusal_dynamics} trace refusal dynamics in standard LLMs. None conducts a systematic modality comparison for commitment detection.

\paragraph{Hidden-State Safety Detection.}
Internal activations can detect safety violations in standard LLMs. \citet{zhou2024alignment} show safety-relevant information distributes across layers, while \citet{qi2025shallow_alignment} find that alignment operates primarily over early output tokens. \citet{arditi2024refusal} identify a single refusal direction, but \citet{zhao2025harmfulness} show harmfulness and refusal are encoded separately. \citet{joad2026refusal_multi} reveal eleven category-specific refusal directions, and \citet{wollschlager2025geometry} formalize multi-dimensionality through concept cones, supporting our finding that single-direction methods are insufficient. TrajGuard~\cite{trajguard} uses Mahalanobis distance from safe trajectories; SafeSwitch~\cite{safeswitch} trains hidden-state classifiers; SIREN~\cite{siren} monitors internal representations during generation; SafeDream~\cite{safedream2026} proposes proactive world models for detection. These methods assume threats from external input manipulation and operate at the token level on instruction-tuned models. The self-jailbreak setting differs because transitions are internal and span multiple reasoning steps. \citet{kadali2025internal} decompose internal layer representations to separate jailbreak from benign prompts, confirming that layer-wise structure carries safety-relevant signal. We reproduce TrajGuard (\cref{sec:negative}) and find its geometric approach fails because discriminative signal resides in minor PCA components.

\paragraph{Probing Methodology and Layer Selection.}
Linear probing has a long history from layer-wise classifiers~\cite{alain2017understanding} through sentence probing~\cite{conneau2018probing} to comprehensive surveys~\cite{belinkov2019analysis,belinkov2022probing}. \citet{hewitt2019control} introduce control tasks to distinguish structure from memorization, which we adopt (\cref{app:control}). \citet{belrose2023leace} establish theoretical limits on linear probe extraction; \citet{elazar2021amnesic} test whether probed information is causally used. \citet{tenney2019bert} show syntactic information in middle layers and semantics in deeper layers. \citet{zhang2025probing_selfverify} apply hidden-state probing to self-verification in reasoning models, providing a complementary perspective on what reasoning-model representations encode. Concurrent work by \citet{alvarez2026transport_geometry} uses hidden-state geometry for step-level hallucination detection, sharing our temporal probing framework but targeting a different phenomenon. \citet{boxo2025linear_probes} show that linear probes can rely on textual evidence rather than deeper representations, motivating our comparison of hidden-state probes against text-based monitors. Despite this foundation, layer selection remains underexamined: most studies select by a single metric without sensitivity analysis. We demonstrate that this practice can reverse a study's central conclusion (\cref{sec:layer-results}).

\paragraph{Change-Point Detection.}
BOCPD~\cite{adams2007bayesian} detects abrupt distributional shifts; CCS~\cite{burns2023discovering} discovers latent truth directions via consistency constraints; RepE~\cite{zou2023representation} identifies concept directions through mean differences. We adapt all three to temporal commitment detection (\cref{sec:negative}). BOCPD and CUSUM fail because commitment is gradual. CCS provides no gain because its consistency objective misaligns with temporal structure. RepE shows inconsistent performance across models (0.756 on QwQ, 0.559 on R1-32B), motivating supervised probes with principled layer selection as the minimal effective methodology.

